  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Behaviour · BEH·08</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Have Theory of Mind?</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Knowledge attribution, gaze following, and the evidence</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens show <strong>knowledge-state attribution</strong> — they treat competitors who witnessed a cache differently from those who did not. Bugnyar & Heinrich (2005) and Bugnyar et al. (2016) provide the core evidence. This is a component of theory of mind but not its full scope. The term "theory of mind" is contested in animal cognition research; the precise question is what mental states ravens represent, and how much of this is genuine mentalising vs learned associative rule-following.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>What "Theory of Mind" Means</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Theory of mind (ToM) is the ability to attribute mental states — beliefs, desires, intentions, knowledge — to others and use those attributions to predict and explain behaviour. It develops in human children around age 3–4.</p>
    <p>In animal cognition research, ToM is usually tested in components:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gaze following:</strong> Does the animal follow another's line of sight?</li>
      <li><strong>Knowledge attribution:</strong> Does the animal distinguish between a knowledgeable and an ignorant competitor?</li>
      <li><strong>False belief understanding:</strong> Can the animal predict what someone will do based on a belief that is wrong?</li>
      <li><strong>Perspective-taking:</strong> Does the animal understand what another can see from their vantage point?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ravens have been tested on several of these components with positive results.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Key Studies</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bugnyar &amp; Heinrich (2005)</strong>: Ravens differentiating knowledgeable from ignorant competitors in food caching. A raven that cached food while watched by a dominant competitor would protect the cache differently than when watched by a bird that was absent (ignorant). This suggests the cacher distinguishes the competitor's knowledge state — a component of ToM. <em>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</em>, 272, 1641–1646.</li>
      <li><strong>Bugnyar et al. (2016)</strong>: The "peephole paradigm" — a raven was able to see a competitor through a small hole, but was tested on its behaviour as if the competitor might have seen it, without direct gaze exchange. Ravens adjusted cache-protection behaviour in response to the mere possibility of being observed. This is a more demanding test. <em>Nature Communications</em>, 7, 10506. DOI: 10.1038/ncomms10506</li>
      <li><strong>Pika &amp; Bugnyar (2011)</strong>: Referential gestures in ravens — ravens used pointing-like gestures toward objects while monitoring a partner's gaze, suggesting awareness of attentional states. <em>Current Biology</em>, 21(20).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Gaze Following</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens follow the gaze of conspecifics and humans — they look in the direction another individual is looking, particularly around barriers. Bugnyar et al. (2004) documented gaze following in ravens, including geometrically correct gaze following around obstacles (looking around a barrier to see what a conspecific is looking at, not just looking in the same direction). This component of perspective-taking is well-established in ravens.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>What Is and Is Not Established</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Honest summary of the evidence:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Well-established:</strong> Ravens follow gaze; ravens distinguish knowledgeable from ignorant competitors; ravens adjust cache-protection behaviour in response to observation</li>
      <li><strong>Demonstrated but contested:</strong> Whether the peephole paradigm (Bugnyar 2016) demonstrates genuine perspective-taking or a learned rule about "holes → might be watched"</li>
      <li><strong>Not yet demonstrated:</strong> Full false-belief understanding (Standard ToM test); ravens have not been shown to predict behaviour based on beliefs they know to be false</li>
      <li><strong>Contested interpretation:</strong> Whether evidence for knowledge attribution reflects genuine mentalising (representing others' mental states) or sophisticated associative learning that mimics mentalising without it</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The honest scientific position is that ravens show some components of ToM to a degree not seen in most animals — but whether this constitutes "true" theory of mind in the philosophical sense remains actively debated.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

